    "title": "Meteor streaks across Ohio skies with explosive boom. See video",
    "text": "March 17, 2026Updated March 18, 2026, 6:19 a.m. ETA piece of the sky fell this morning.A fireball seen throughout Ohio around 9 a.m. March 17 was a meteor, according to the National Weather Service of Cleveland.According to the American Meteorological Society's fireball event tracker, there have been 140 reports from people who saw the fireball in Ohio, Indiana, Kentucky, Michigan, New York, Pennsylvania, Virginia, Illinois, Delaware, Maryland, the District of Columbia, and Canada.In eastern Ohio, the meteor was also heard.\"Heard the loudest boom just now in northeast OH. People heard at as far as Avon. What was that? Meteor?\" posted @Jacecraftmiller on X, tagging the National Weather Service of Cleveland.In a reply to the post, the National Weather Service said on X, \"The latest GLM imagery (1301Z) does suggest that the boom was a result of a meteor.\"Reports of the loud noise stretched to western Pennsylvania, according to the National Weather Service of Pittsburgh.Jared Rackley, an employee at the weather service in Pittsburgh captured the meteor on video.What is a meteor, a meteoroid and a meteorite?Three different terms are used to describe the same object at different points in its journey, according to Cincinnati Observatory astronomer Wes Ryle. Meteoroid is a term for a piece of rocky debris out in space that has not encountered Earth or its atmosphere yet. Meteor is the term for that rocky debris coming down through Earth’s atmosphere. At this point, the debris is traveling at such high speeds that it creates friction with the atmosphere and causes a glow, forming the streak we see across the sky. The debris is called a meteorite if it actually hits the surface.“The thing we saw can be described as a meteor,” Ryle said. “It was definitely bright enough to be called a fireball as well.”Like micrometeorites, meteors are also happening all the time, according to Ryle, but it can be hard to see them. If it’s dark outside, the moon isn’t up, and you’re away from the city lights, you can maybe see three or four meteors in an hour. This story may be updated.",
